(p/'README.md').write_text('# Actual CM210 native norm obstruction and nontrivial classes\n\nFour new sources extend all 1,188 exact predecessor sources. Compute the actual native algebra norm on the full O_K as r²+210s² using the already constructed full integer basis and native norm transport. Prove universally that a norm below210 must be a square of an integer, then exclude the fourteen nonsquare proper divisors2,3,5,6,7,10,14,15,21,30,35,42,70,105.\n\nConsequently the actual native ideals constructed for these divisors are not principal. In particular each of the four original prime classes2,3,5,7 is nontrivial in the native ClassGroup O_K. Together with the preceding square theorem, these are genuine nontrivial two-torsion classes. Pairwise independence, all-word kernel and full class-group exhaustion are not yet claimed. Neither CM point residue fields nor reciprocity follows.\n\nIndependent source review/kernel replay remain pending. No local Lean, new caps or frozen edits.\n')
(p/'DERIVATION.md').write_text('# Native norm, universal obstruction and exact class consequence\n\nThe native integer algebra equivalence maps the actual quadratic basis to the full native integer ring. Algebra.norm_eq_of_algEquiv and norm_eq_matrix_det compute its norm from [[r,-210s],[s,r]], giving r²+210s². This is the actual native algebra norm, and Ideal.absNorm_span_singleton gives the norm of an actual principal ideal as its absolute value. The form is nonnegative, so the native integer absolute-value conversion removes natAbs legitimately.\n\nFor arbitrary integer coordinates, suppose r²+210s²=d<210. Nonnegativity of r² forces s²<1. Since s² is a nonnegative integer, it is zero, hence s=0. Therefore r²=d. This universal argument has no bound on the input coordinates. Each of the fourteen listed proper divisors is independently certified nonsquare: any square equal to one of them has -10<=r<=10 because d<=105, then a complete finite integer-case proof rejects all possibilities.\n\nIf an actual native ideal of norm d were principal, choose its native Submodule.IsPrincipal generator, pull it through the actual full integer-ring equivalence, and apply the produced norm formula. The universal obstruction contradicts its norm. Apply this to the exact native reduction ideals already constructed for all fourteen divisors, whose norms were proved from their literal quotient ring equivalences. Finally use ClassGroup.mk0_eq_one_iff on the original four nonzero ideals to prove their actual native classes are not one.\n\nThis does not yet identify products of different prime ideals or exhaust the native class group. Those require separately produced ideal-product equalities and Minkowski classification. No arithmetic Galois action is substituted for the analytic labels.\n')
(p/'regress_cm_small_norm_rebuild.py').write_text("from pathlib import Path\nfrom itertools import product\nimport json\nD=[2,3,5,6,7,10,14,15,21,30,35,42,70,105]\nassert all(0<d<210 and 210%d==0 for d in D)\nassert all(r*r!=d for d,r in product(D,range(-10,11)))\ncount=0\nfor r,s in product(range(-300,301),repeat=2):\n N=r*r+210*s*s\n if N<210:assert s==0 and N==r*r\n assert N not in D\n count+=1\n# Necessary nonsquare and strict-bound assumptions cannot be dropped.\nassert 2*2==4 and 0*0+210*1*1==210\nout={'status':'PASS','universal_norm_stress_cases':count,'complete_small_square_cases':14*21,'proper_divisor_checks':14,'negative_controls':2,'scope':'Exact regression only; universal Lean source proof is separate and uncompiled.'}\nPath(__file__).with_name('REGRESSION.json').write_text(json.dumps(out,indent=2)+'\\n');print(out)\n")
